00:33:26.507 --> 00:33:48.463
And like our life's journey and our, yeah, journey that we take through life is taking things into our consciousness. But when we do something first, we are the fool. We are very awkward. We're doing it wrong and we have to do it multiple times to get it right.

00:33:49.650 --> 00:34:11.107
Like for example, speaking to a girl, like as a guy to a girl for the first time, this is a nerve-wracking, painful experience that you somehow can get better at. But this is what it means. Like it's limited and it can expand over time. That's a hopeful thing, but it takes a lot of effort.

00:34:12.454 --> 00:34:31.146
Of course, when we reference to the things that we know and that we can do and that we experience, that's also always directed into the past. So consciousness for Jung means only the things we already have and know, but he says like, oh yeah, there's a lot of stuff in the future, a lot of stuff we will encounter and change into, but that's in the unconscious.

00:34:31.194 --> 00:34:35.747
This is out of grasp for us. This is not yet available.

00:34:38.579 --> 00:35:02.135
And this is why he says when he has people in therapy, when they have problems, which are problems now and very often concern their future, people regress into their past and start thinking incredibly hard about their past because this is the stuff which is immediate available. Because having it in consciousness is always easier than integrating new stuff into consciousness.

00:35:02.825 --> 00:35:14.113
So what he would see, for example, that his patients would become very, very interested in their childhood and like their family home and their past and trying to find the answers in the past.

00:35:14.161 --> 00:35:32.261
And he says there is value in there, of course, maybe there, there are unprocessed things there, but at the same time, like, yeah, the future is in front at the fearful place, like the future is uncertain and novel and potentially pretty painful, but also there's all where the good things are.

00:35:35.060 --> 00:35:57.801
By consciousness being adaptable and willfully oriented towards a direction, we become what Jung says, one-sided, which means we are focusing on very small parts of reality. Jung, for example, sees like four functions, how information comes into ego and consciousness.

00:35:58.370 --> 00:36:23.348
So how does new stuff come into ego and consciousness? And he sees there sensation, thinking, feeling, and intuition. This is the basis for his typology model. Sensation saying there is something, thinking, labeling it. For example, oh, that's a tree. And feeling is, do I like the tree or don't like the tree? And intuition would be, oh, what can I do out of the tree?

00:36:23.573 --> 00:36:42.527
I can make some nice furniture, for example. And he says human beings, as we are born, we're pretty limited and we have like a favorite function, like we have a favorite hand. And of course we will always try to do things first with our favorite hand, meaning the other one does not get used. It's not so sophisticated. It's not so elaborate.

00:36:43.457 --> 00:37:03.654
And Jung says, ah, well, you're a complete human being. You should be able to use all your different functions. So he says, you can get incredibly far by thinking incredibly far. You can make a life, a career out of it. But at a certain point, the unconscious, your psyche will come knocking and saying, yeah, but you should also take care of the feeling.

00:37:04.087 --> 00:37:23.193
And you would hit certain roadblocks because you are only approaching things in a thinking manner. And you have to do a different tool, but this is a tool you are not sophisticated in feeling, sensation, or intuition. And you need to learn to become the fool again and have to stumble a lot, and it will be awkward and it will be painful.

00:37:23.578 --> 00:37:41.581
But on the same side, it's like if you do it willfully, you will develop in this direction. You will integrate those things and you will become a more complete human being and you're able to do more things. And most important, like You can become the person you're supposed to be, like you're destined to be.

00:37:43.620 --> 00:38:01.615
Yeah, this one-sidedness means also incomplete, but this also has to do with like the impressions that we get from the outside or from the inside. We're missing really a lot of things. Like consciousness can hold and we can pay attention to a couple of things at the same time.

00:38:02.012 --> 00:38:11.577
And we might have the impression that this is then the whole world and this is everything there is, but at the same time, we are missing a tremendous amount of stuff.

00:38:12.427 --> 00:38:34.454
So, but how does the psyche, according to Jung, deal with the fact that we have a limited, small, unsophisticated consciousness that's struggling all the time, but still can adapt to things and still find a way how to reorientate ourselves? And he says that the function of the unconscious. So I before mentioned the net, right? The net of consciousness.

00:38:36.417 --> 00:38:50.786
I want to build on this metaphor. When you want to fish, right? And this is what like fish trawlers do in the open ocean. They have a net and they try to catch the fish. So they're going with the net through the water and they take what they can take.

00:38:51.468 --> 00:39:14.383
So when you have a very a net with very big holes and a huge gap between the threads, you can catch big fishes, but like small fishes, they will evade you. And similar is that with consciousness. You can only catch the things that you have the necessary consciousness for to connect it.

00:39:15.222 --> 00:39:33.239
Like if you have a very simple consciousness, then the holes are very big where a lot of stuff gets through that you do not know about it because you cannot, you can only know about the things that are related to your consciousness to see and experience them. But as you get more and more sophisticated, you get notice more smaller and smaller things.

00:39:34.220 --> 00:39:52.946
For example, when you try to figure out what's wrong with your car, like you will miss a lot of stuff, but you can go to a car mechanic and they can maybe just even listen to it and say like, oh yeah, that's There's something wrong in this part and they can pinpoint it exactly because they have the right net. They can pick up the correct things.

00:39:53.603 --> 00:40:05.647
Or like when you don't know what to do with your life, you can go to a therapist and they can notice stuff that you don't notice about your life or about how you talk about things to say like, yeah, well, you're not talking about that.

00:40:07.010 --> 00:40:29.931
And there Jung says, this is a function of a therapist or like a friend or a partner in interpersonal relationships, pointing out things that you're missing. Because as I said, in consciousness, you say like, oh yeah, I can see the whole world. Now I know everything. I can make sense of everything. But the chances are zero that this is true.

00:40:30.060 --> 00:40:51.153
Like you can only deal with parts at certain times, but most things are just problems you do not even recognize as problems. I say just wash past you. But it is not only external people that fill this function, but also the unconscious, the psyche. And this is what I mentioned, this old ancient past that has seen everything before.

00:40:51.786 --> 00:41:15.567
So it will pick up on certain things and then will push things to consciousness to make it aware of that. And Carl Gustav Jung says this is what's happening in dreams. So when we dream, It's like you've had your whole day walking around and you did it with someone else and you saw and experienced everything that you saw and experienced, but you missed a lot of things.

00:41:15.759 --> 00:41:29.391
And there was this other person that picked up on all these things. And when you go to sleep, the psyche then informs you about all the things that you missed that happened inside, also outside. You know, there's experience like when you go to a new city with someone you, you even know.

00:41:30.434 --> 00:41:50.312
They will notice completely different things about the city and they will point it out like, oh, look here, that's a nice building. Oh, be careful here. And this is what Jung says the psyche does. And this is why he says, oh, you have to kind of build a relationship with that and be aware of that and be open to that, not closed down and say like, I know everything.

00:41:51.227 --> 00:42:13.367
There's no need to change. I don't need to focus. I don't need a goal, whatever. But rather, okay, there's something inside me that could point out things to me that are new to me. And this is the strange premise of psychoanalysis to say human beings are not the master in their own house, but there are a lot of things happening that we are not aware of.

00:42:14.106 --> 00:42:29.222
And Jung says this very often, like, for us, the world looks like consciousness, but at the same time, consciousness is way, way, way, way smaller than all the unconscious stuff that's happening around us, but also that is happening inside of us.

00:42:31.476 --> 00:42:47.558
And dreaming and sleeping is already the right cue for the next point, which is when we have consciousness and we have to focus and pay attention, we get tired very easily. Like if we know something, we can do it automatic. We can drive hours without thinking.

00:42:48.118 --> 00:43:07.506
But if we do it first, we can do it with for 5 minutes without being completely exhausted and sweating and all these different things. So every day for 8 hours and sometimes more, we have to rest just to recharge our consciousness to be then able again to adjust and adapt to the world and things that are happening there.

00:43:09.212 --> 00:43:27.885
And you have this, for example, with athletes or like people that work very hard cognitively, that they can do the focus part and the strenuous part for a couple of hours, but then they need to rest.

00:43:27.981 --> 00:43:47.819
Like I heard that even for like professional musicians, they would train like crazy for 3 hours and then they would go to sleep and then they would do it again just to recharge again. So yeah. We are easily exhaustible. We're also very easily distractible. And today it's very easy with social media and the internet that something grabs our attention and bam, we are gone.

00:43:48.188 --> 00:44:07.315
Suddenly we don't have a plan anymore. Suddenly we don't have a focus anymore. And it's then weak small consciousness being overwhelmed with all the things that are happening around it. And yeah, of course, because something is in your consciousness doesn't mean you fully comprehend it.

00:44:07.530 --> 00:44:20.641
You can also completely misunderstand the, the world or situation, and being able to differentiate that and to separate that and reflect on that is a skill that needs to be learned.

00:44:21.306 --> 00:44:44.624
And especially like in interpersonal communication, this is why we then talk and give feedback to understand like, okay, why is their problem right now. So this is why we ask questions. And a good example that I heard recently was like, oh, I thought you're angry, but actually you're afraid.

00:44:45.463 --> 00:45:07.662
So this reflecting, reframing, and so all these functions of consciousness are an important part that we kind of have to do because consciousness is limited, incomplete, very easily exhaustible. So it's like constant work. We constantly have to work on this ship that keeps on sailing while water is leaking in all the time.

00:45:10.230 --> 00:45:26.408
And I have some quotes. Carl Gustav Jung: Man knows no more than his consciousness, and he knows himself only as far as it extends. That's what I mean. This world as we see it, we think it's complete, but actually we're missing a lot. So like humility is a good first step.

00:45:28.865 --> 00:45:49.630
For that, by confining our activity to a single sphere, we have handed ourselves over to a master who is not infrequently to end up by suppressing the rest of our capacities. And this is what I meant with when we focus on one thing, we leave out a lot of other things. So when you have high career people that only work, work, work, work, they have miserable lives. Why?

00:45:50.495 --> 00:46:10.626
They don't have meaningful relationships because that stuff takes time and they did not take the time. So to be a complete human being, you have to pay attention to a lot of things and tend a lot of plates at the same time. So it's a blessing and a curse that we can focus on one thing, but very likely we are missing another thing and we have to then catch up.

00:46:10.626 --> 00:46:30.262
And consciousness always succumbs to the delusion that it developed out of itself. The consciousness and ego that Jung sees is an a phenomena of the psyche and it emerges and we do not exactly know why, but it pops up, it develops, it does its thing.

00:46:31.160 --> 00:46:47.393
And this is how, yeah, we start our life and we start our experiences. So this is then our journey through life, starting from a state of complete unconsciousness, uh, as a child coming up.

00:46:47.730 --> 00:47:09.729
With this like small island of ego and consciousness in this big sea of the unconscious, developing, fortifying, learning, adapting, and experiences all these horrible things, but also to overcome them and grow and collect all the things that is us. And this is what Jung calls individuation.

00:47:10.150 --> 00:47:32.116
And individuation, you have, and basically all concepts that Jung talks about, And this is, this becoming an individual, so becoming unique, but also to become separated of other things, of external influences, internal influences, which does not mean to suppress them or ignore them, but rather learning how to integrate them, learning how to handle them.

00:47:32.677 --> 00:47:39.939
And this is a very nice notion that Jung has about one's life and how one should live one's life.

00:47:40.516 --> 00:48:00.459
He says you're starting out and like all the future things about you are in the unconscious and your life and your individual journey is about collecting those things and bringing them home, like returning back and combining the things, gathering the things that should be combined and gathered.

00:48:01.310 --> 00:48:24.325
And this is then the life one ideally lives and brings together, and that makes one what one is. And this would conclude my presentation for Jung's idea of consciousness and all the different aspects he saw there and the different interactions he saw, especially with the unconscious.

00:48:25.777 --> 00:48:37.510
And I want to thank you for your attention, and I hope you had fun, and I hope you learned something. And I'm now really curious about the discussion part. Thank you.

